WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.780
Let’s review aldehyde and ketone reactivity,
because this will tell us a lot about how

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.750
and why enols and enolates form.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:14.789
Here is acetone, a simple ketone.

00:00:14.789 --> 00:00:19.700
Oxygen is more electronegative than carbon,
so the oxygen that are shared in this bond

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:26.070
are pulled toward the oxygen; they're whizzing
around in this bond but because oxygen attracts

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:33.430
the electrons more we get a dipole in this
molecule.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:38.660
We can also show a resonance form for acetone
that puts a formal negative charge on oxygen

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.250
and a positive charge on carbon.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:45.220
This contributes significantly to the carbonyl
group’s reactivity.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:50.860
So, when an electron rich atom bonds to a
carbonyl, it's going to do so at the delta

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.079
positive carbon atom.

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:58.239
In a Grignard reaction, the Grignard carbon
has lots of negative charge concentrated on

00:00:58.239 --> 00:00:59.239
it.

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:04.670
So, the nucleophilic carbon of the Grignard
reagent is going to attack at the electrophilic

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:14.310
carbon atom and pushing the electrons up onto
oxygen Then a second stage of the reaction

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:21.950
is carried out, where dilute acid is added
to give the oxygen a proton and form an alcohol.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:35.600
Acid-catalyzed additions of nucleophiles are
also possible with aldehydes and ketones.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:41.780
Since the oxygen atom of the carbonyl is electron
rich it can attack a proton.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:46.850
Once it's protonated, the positive charge
on oxygen makes the adjacent carbon atom even

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.530
more electropositive.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:54.220
In the reaction I've shown here, the nucleophile
is one of the oxygen atoms of the molecule

00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:00.450
below the arrow, ethylene glycol.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:04.890
In several steps, we eventually get bonding
of both oxygen atoms of ethylene glycol to

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:10.069
the carbonyl carbon, forming this functional
group, called a ketal.

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:16.090
The dipole in acetone also affects the hydrogen
atoms that are adjacent to the carbonyl.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:23.370
They feel the pull of the dipole in the molecule,
and consequently become more acidic with a

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.810
pKa of 27.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:30.250
Now, this might not seem too acidic, but compared
to a regular unsubstituted hydrocarbon, which

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:38.250
has a pKa of around 50, this is a substantial
decrease.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:42.540
The acidity of the alpha protons allows them
to be deprotonated.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:47.870
Sodium hydroxide can attack the acidic proton,
and we push electrons all the way through

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:57.530
the carbonyl to show how formation of the
anion is stabilized by conjugation.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:02.270
There is another resonance form that puts
the negative charge on carbon—so an enolate

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:04.840
is a carbon nucleophile.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:12.160
We went from a compound that undergoes nucleophilic
attack to something that can do the attacking!

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:17.840
A compound with analogous reactivity, called
an enol, can be formed under acid-catalyzed

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.230
conditions.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:23.950
The mechanism begins the same as the acid
catalyzed addition we showed above, where

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:30.470
the electrons on the delta negative oxygen
can attack a proton on sulfuric acid, in this

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:31.900
case.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:37.050
Protonation of the carbonyl oxygen makes the
alpha hydrogens even more acidic, and they

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.460
can be deprotonated.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:44.659
This is reasonable because the conjugate base
of sulfuric acid is highly resonance stabilized

00:03:44.659 --> 00:03:50.030
and isn’t very nucleophilic; typically there's
a bit of water even in the most concentrated

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:51.790
solutions of acid.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:04:00.470
So, under acidic conditions, we formed an
enol, which is also a nucleophile.

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:05.090
Now that we’ve looked at how enols and enolates
are formed, let’s look briefly at their

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:06.519
reactivity.

00:04:06.519 --> 00:04:09.930
The first reaction we'll look at is alpha
halogenation.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:16.560
This is going to introduce a halogen atom
at the carbon adjacent to the carbonyl.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:23.080
We'll carry out this reaction under acidic
conditions, using acetic acid and bromine.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.660
In the first step, our enol is generated.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:32.610
Then, a lone pair on oxygen can push back
down, allowing the double bond of the enol

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:36.729
to attack bromine, displacing Br minus.

00:04:36.729 --> 00:04:43.189
Now, we get a positive charge on oxygen, which
can be deprotonated with acetate, which we

00:04:43.189 --> 00:04:50.099
formed when we protonated our carbonyl compound
to form our enol in the first place.

00:04:50.099 --> 00:04:55.759
So, why are these alpha halogenated carbonyl
compounds useful?

00:04:55.759 --> 00:05:01.979
Well, they're great substrates for SN2 reactions,
and we can displace the bromine with a variety

00:05:01.979 --> 00:05:02.979
of nucleophiles.

00:05:02.979 --> 00:05:09.319
Here, I’m showing a reaction with dimethylamine
to make an alpha amino compound.

00:05:09.319 --> 00:05:14.430
We can also form carbon–carbon bonds using
enols and enolates, which is very important

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:19.999
in synthetic organic chemistry where the goal
is to build up more complex carbon skeletons

00:05:19.999 --> 00:05:22.300
from simpler molecules.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:27.990
To show this alkylation reaction, I’m going
to first deprotonate acetone to form an enolate.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:31.610
Now, we need a little bit of fancier chemistry
here.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:36.240
The enolate/enol forming reactions I’ve
shown above are actually in equilibrium when

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.569
made with NaOH or sulfuric acid.

00:05:39.569 --> 00:05:44.219
But to make this alkylation reaction proceed
with any sort of control, we're going to need

00:05:44.219 --> 00:05:46.650
some fancier chemistry here.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:53.789
We're going to use lithium diisopropylamide,
or LDA, to generate this enolate completely.

00:05:53.789 --> 00:05:58.659
LDA has two isopropyl groups and a negative
charge on nitrogen.

00:05:58.659 --> 00:06:00.610
This makes it a very hindered base.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:06.800
So, it can't attack at our carbonyl, as amines
are known to do, instead it's big and bulky

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.300
and it can only get at this alpha hydrogen.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:10.300
we need to generate our enolate completely,
so we’re going to use a special base called

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.300
LDA, or lithium diisopropylamide.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:15.599
It's is also much more basic than sodium hydroxide,
so we can treat our compound with one equivalent

00:06:15.599 --> 00:06:19.729
of LDA and generate our enolate completely.

00:06:19.729 --> 00:06:25.020
Now, in a second step, we can add in an alkyl
halide.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:28.569
Since we used up all of our LDA, we don’t
have to worry at all about our base reacting

00:06:28.569 --> 00:06:30.360
with the bromide.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:35.659
The enolate attacks at the electropositive
carbon next to the bromide leaving group,

00:06:35.659 --> 00:06:41.639
and our final molecule extends our carbon
chain by three carbon atoms.

00:06:41.639 --> 00:06:46.559
This reaction works best with alkyl halides
that are not hindered, so primary alkyl halides

00:06:46.559 --> 00:06:49.620
are a good choice for this reaction.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:54.189
Before we wrap up, I do need to mention that
enolate alkylation has lots of limitations

00:06:54.189 --> 00:06:59.479
and, in another lesson, we will go over these
limitations and how to work around them.

